{"requirements":[{"id":"serve-inventory-8181","requirement":"Serve the inventory application on node1 over TCP port 8181.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Apache is enabled and active, listens on all interfaces at TCP 8181, and returns HTTP 200 with the Inventory Application content. The listener and application remained available after a real node restart; the host firewall permits 8181 in runtime and permanent configuration.","evidence_ids":["cmd-7e250f6f92c24e6c","cmd-8cb628bc87d4463e","cmd-5469357a5d2b4ee0","cmd-92732c8d2e854907","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"mac-enforcing","requirement":"Keep the system's mandatory access control in enforcing mode while serving the application.","status":"satisfied","summary":"SELinux remained Enforcing. TCP 8181 is assigned http_port_t, the application content has httpd_sys_content_t, and the running Apache processes execute in httpd_t, including after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0f2b2af14bf4a69","cmd-0a2d33d8185c49aa","cmd-7e250f6f92c24e6c","cmd-92732c8d2e854907"]},{"id":"legacy-platform-compatibility","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The platform remained RHEL 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 before and after execution. Apache 2.4.62 was installed from the configured RHEL 9 repository and identifies Red Hat as its vendor.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9a24a53f65ad47a0","cmd-73473241f0a945f5","cmd-8cb628bc87d4463e","cmd-92732c8d2e854907","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.94,"summary":"All substantive system mutations had clear roles: installing Apache, deploying and labeling content, configuring SELinux and the firewall, enabling the service, and performing the mandatory restart. No failed services or package damage were observed afterward. Two small temporary artifacts were retained: /tmp/inventory-reboot.log from the restart protocol and /tmp/inventory-response.html from final HTTP evidence collection. Both were bounded, low-risk residues but were not cleaned up. No causally unrelated troubleshooting mutation was identified.","evidence_ids":["cmd-73473241f0a945f5","cmd-c0f2b2af14bf4a69","cmd-0a2d33d8185c49aa","cmd-5469357a5d2b4ee0","cmd-7e250f6f92c24e6c","cmd-fcadaa284acb4770","cmd-92732c8d2e854907","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for node1: it records a healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, restart acknowledgement, observed connection interruption, a different post-restart boot ID, and recovered service, application content, SELinux, firewall, OS, and kernel state. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["The HTTP request was issued locally on node1 against its managed IP rather than from a separate client, although the all-interface listener and firewall observations strongly support network accessibility.","The bounded lifecycle snapshots do not inventory /etc/httpd, so they cannot independently determine whether the configuration backup created at /etc/httpd/conf/httpd.conf.pre-inventory remained after completion."]}
